1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,320
Hello everyone and welcome back to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:05,640 --> 00:00:06,080
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:06,480 --> 00:00:11,080
My name is Catherine and today we've got a very nerve-wracking situation for you.

4
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:12,040
That's right.

5
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:15,560
We are going to meet the in-laws.

6
00:00:15,560 --> 00:00:16,560
Meeting the in-laws.

7
00:00:16,560 --> 00:00:17,800
So who are in-laws?

8
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:21,480
These are the parents of your husband or wife.

9
00:00:21,960 --> 00:00:22,960
Right.

10
00:00:22,960 --> 00:00:26,720
So in this case, you have the mother-in-law and your father-in-law.

11
00:00:26,720 --> 00:00:26,880
That's right.

12
00:00:27,720 --> 00:00:29,940
We're meeting these people for the very first time.

13
00:00:30,120 --> 00:00:34,420
You can imagine how nervous the man is who's meeting his future wife's parents.

14
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:37,900
Let's learn all about what they talk about in today's dialogue.

15
00:00:46,700 --> 00:00:49,780
Mother, father, I'd like to introduce you to my fiancé, Bob.

16
00:00:50,260 --> 00:00:51,000
Hello, Bob.

17
00:00:51,760 --> 00:00:52,760
Welcome.

18
00:00:52,760 --> 00:00:53,120
Thanks for having me.

19
00:00:53,760 --> 00:00:54,540
Nice to meet the both of you.

20
00:00:54,780 --> 00:00:55,560
I've heard so much.

21
00:00:56,520 --> 00:01:01,040
So Cindy told you about bringing home her last boyfriend then.

22
00:01:01,040 --> 00:01:01,220
That idiot.

23
00:01:02,040 --> 00:01:03,040
Shhh, Thurston.

24
00:01:03,040 --> 00:01:03,860
You're going to scare the poor boy.

25
00:01:04,320 --> 00:01:05,280
Come in and have a drink.

26
00:01:05,920 --> 00:01:07,240
Dinner will be on in just a bit.

27
00:01:07,240 --> 00:01:09,240
What will you be having?

28
00:01:09,240 --> 00:01:10,280
Whiskey, bourbon?

29
00:01:10,280 --> 00:01:10,580
Pick your poison.

30
00:01:11,460 --> 00:01:13,680
If you have a lemonade, that'd be great.

31
00:01:14,120 --> 00:01:15,120
Lemonade?

32
00:01:15,120 --> 00:01:16,120
Why, sure.

33
00:01:16,120 --> 00:01:17,120
There's some in the fridge.

34
00:01:17,120 --> 00:01:18,420
Mother makes her own lemonade from scratch.

35
00:01:19,160 --> 00:01:19,520
It's the best.

36
00:01:20,320 --> 00:01:21,560
So what are your hobbies, son?

37
00:01:22,080 --> 00:01:23,720
If you want, we can do some hunting tomorrow.

38
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:26,610
I've just picked up a new rifle I've been meaning to try out.

39
00:01:26,680 --> 00:01:27,680
It should be a real hoot.

40
00:01:27,790 --> 00:01:31,470
Um, I'm not really, uh, I don't really hunt.

41
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:32,610
You don't hunt?

42
00:01:33,120 --> 00:01:34,840
Well, I'll beat damn.

43
00:01:34,840 --> 00:01:36,530
Bob is an animal rights activist.

44
00:01:37,010 --> 00:01:38,390
He doesn't believe in harming animals.

45
00:01:39,350 --> 00:01:39,650
Dinner's ready.

46
00:01:40,110 --> 00:01:42,070
Let's go out to the patio where the pig is roasting.

47
00:01:42,910 --> 00:01:43,570
Roast pig?

48
00:01:44,400 --> 00:01:47,400
I'm a vegetarian.

49
00:01:50,250 --> 00:01:51,390
All right, we're back.

50
00:01:51,680 --> 00:01:53,530
So a very awkward situation.

51
00:01:54,160 --> 00:01:58,360
I think they might have a little bit of a problems in the future.

52
00:01:58,590 --> 00:02:05,430
Yeah, there's there are a lot of cultural differences between the characters, the son-in-law and the new parents, the father.

53
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:08,110
So it's really interesting.

54
00:02:08,480 --> 00:02:13,710
Why don't we start off by looking at a couple of different words on language takeaway.

55
00:02:16,180 --> 00:02:17,160
Language takeaway.

56
00:02:18,420 --> 00:02:22,900
So first of all, we have the two characters, Cindy and Bob, who are getting married.

57
00:02:23,380 --> 00:02:26,800
So we call them, we call Bob, Cindy's fiance.

58
00:02:26,800 --> 00:02:27,800
Right.

59
00:02:27,800 --> 00:02:29,560
So my fiance.

60
00:02:29,560 --> 00:02:34,360
And it's, uh, it comes from French and it means to the person that you're going to get married to.

61
00:02:34,360 --> 00:02:34,580
That's right.

62
00:02:35,360 --> 00:02:36,560
The person you've decided to get married to.

63
00:02:36,840 --> 00:02:38,800
So we say fiance.

64
00:02:38,800 --> 00:02:41,320
We can say the same word for a man or a woman.

65
00:02:41,320 --> 00:02:43,000
So he is my fiance.

66
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:44,360
She is my fiance.

67
00:02:44,360 --> 00:02:45,360
Okay.

68
00:02:45,360 --> 00:02:46,360
My fiance.

69
00:02:46,360 --> 00:02:47,360
Very good.

70
00:02:47,360 --> 00:02:57,460
So Cindy introduces her fiance, Bob, to her parents and they start talking a little bit and her dad, Thurston, invites him to go do some hunting tomorrow.

71
00:02:58,800 --> 00:02:58,960
That's right.

72
00:02:59,800 --> 00:03:05,840
So hunting is a sport in America and lots of places in the world where you shoot animals.

73
00:03:05,840 --> 00:03:06,840
Okay.

74
00:03:06,840 --> 00:03:15,970
So you go in the woods, you wear brown and green colors and you shoot animals to take them home and skin them and eat them or to keep them as trophies.

75
00:03:16,240 --> 00:03:17,240
All right.

76
00:03:17,240 --> 00:03:23,150
So you go hunting and well, the way that you shoot these animals is you need to have a rifle.

77
00:03:23,730 --> 00:03:24,090
A rifle.

78
00:03:24,490 --> 00:03:27,030
So you could also call it a hunting rifle in this case.

79
00:03:27,470 --> 00:03:30,290
A rifle is a gun, but it's a very long gun.

80
00:03:30,650 --> 00:03:30,890
Right.

81
00:03:31,350 --> 00:03:33,710
So it's not a little gun that you just hold in your hand.

82
00:03:33,850 --> 00:03:34,810
It's very long.

83
00:03:35,240 --> 00:03:36,850
It'll be maybe like half a meter, a meter long.

84
00:03:37,360 --> 00:03:37,620
That's right.

85
00:03:38,360 --> 00:03:45,980
And so oftentimes people, when they're hunting, they lie on the ground and they wait for the animals to pass and they hide themselves and then they shoot from a long distance.

86
00:03:46,600 --> 00:03:47,600
Right.

87
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:48,480
So he's talking about hunting.

88
00:03:48,920 --> 00:03:52,340
He's got a new rifle and he says, yeah, we should definitely go.

89
00:03:52,560 --> 00:03:54,600
It should be a real hoot.

90
00:03:54,600 --> 00:03:55,510
Should be a real hoot.

91
00:03:55,950 --> 00:03:59,270
This is a very fun spoken English phrase.

92
00:03:59,970 --> 00:04:03,270
It's said more by older people than by younger people.

93
00:04:03,610 --> 00:04:05,350
So this is why the father says it in the dialogue.

94
00:04:05,950 --> 00:04:08,470
A hoot should be a real good time, real fun.

95
00:04:09,480 --> 00:04:12,730
So how can you can use this like, oh, the party last night was a real hoot.

96
00:04:13,080 --> 00:04:14,080
Yeah.

97
00:04:14,080 --> 00:04:15,080
You can say that.

98
00:04:15,080 --> 00:04:16,230
You can say or she's a real hoot.

99
00:04:16,520 --> 00:04:17,170
She's really funny.

100
00:04:17,520 --> 00:04:18,520
Okay.

101
00:04:18,520 --> 00:04:19,520
But it's really enjoyable.

102
00:04:19,520 --> 00:04:23,680
This is a little bit more like spoken and for used by maybe older people.

103
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:25,520
My mother would say that I wouldn't say this.

104
00:04:25,720 --> 00:04:28,520
So, oh my goodness, it was a real hoot.

105
00:04:28,520 --> 00:04:29,520
Yeah.

106
00:04:29,520 --> 00:04:30,520
All right.

107
00:04:30,520 --> 00:04:33,040
So he's talking about hunting, how much fun they would have.

108
00:04:33,800 --> 00:04:40,160
And unfortunately, Bob is an animal rights activist.

109
00:04:40,160 --> 00:04:40,620
What does this mean?

110
00:04:41,160 --> 00:04:46,800
So an animal rights activist is someone who is very concerned with protecting the rights of animals.

111
00:04:46,800 --> 00:05:01,490
So for example, if I'm an animal rights activist, maybe I will protest people who wear fur or perhaps I will also try and secure land for a natural wildlife preserve so that animals can live in the wild.

112
00:05:01,720 --> 00:05:02,930
There's not houses or cars.

113
00:05:03,800 --> 00:05:10,080
So animal rights activists are people who try to make sure that animals are protected from human beings.

114
00:05:10,080 --> 00:05:11,070
Okay, very good.

115
00:05:11,250 --> 00:05:13,220
And this is what Bob is.

116
00:05:13,300 --> 00:05:15,700
So obviously Bob is not really into hunting, right?

117
00:05:15,920 --> 00:05:16,980
I'm guessing no.

118
00:05:17,280 --> 00:05:18,560
These are two opposites.

119
00:05:18,800 --> 00:05:24,040
You have people who like to kill animals and people who want to protect the lives of animals.

120
00:05:24,040 --> 00:05:25,040
Right.

121
00:05:25,040 --> 00:05:25,180
Okay.

122
00:05:26,040 --> 00:05:29,520
Well, and to finish off our language takeaway, dinner is ready.

123
00:05:29,920 --> 00:05:31,960
So let's go out to the patio.

124
00:05:32,940 --> 00:05:33,220
Okay.

125
00:05:33,440 --> 00:05:39,120
A patio is a space outside the house, usually in the back that's paved.

126
00:05:39,120 --> 00:05:42,430
So there's like cement or bricks or some kind of tile.

127
00:05:42,910 --> 00:05:46,350
And that's where you have a table and chairs or some place to sit and rest.

128
00:05:46,650 --> 00:05:47,550
This is where you have a grill.

129
00:05:48,010 --> 00:05:49,910
So it's an outdoor area, but it's not grass.

130
00:05:50,360 --> 00:05:50,510
Okay.

131
00:05:51,360 --> 00:05:52,130
So it's not your backyard.

132
00:05:52,360 --> 00:05:54,260
Oh, it could be in your backyard.

133
00:05:54,270 --> 00:05:56,590
The patio could be in the backyard.

134
00:05:56,750 --> 00:05:57,230
That's right.

135
00:05:57,410 --> 00:05:59,090
But the ground in the patio is not grass.

136
00:05:59,350 --> 00:06:02,550
It's some kind of material like cement or brick or wood.

137
00:06:03,030 --> 00:06:03,890
Okay, very good.

138
00:06:04,090 --> 00:06:05,030
So great words there.

139
00:06:05,210 --> 00:06:09,230
Why don't we listen to the dialogue again and we'll be back in a bit with Fluency Builder.

140
00:06:11,440 --> 00:06:13,600
All right, we're back.

141
00:06:13,600 --> 00:06:16,190
So we have three great phrases here on Fluency Builder.

142
00:06:16,370 --> 00:06:17,890
Why don't we start off with the first one?

143
00:06:18,790 --> 00:06:26,390
So the first one, we have to go back to the very beginning of this dialogue and think about these people who are introducing each other for the very first time.

144
00:06:27,080 --> 00:06:29,530
Cindy says, I want to introduce you to my fiance, Bob.

145
00:06:30,400 --> 00:06:34,070
Bob says to Cindy's parents, I've heard so much.

146
00:06:34,480 --> 00:06:35,790
I've heard so much.

147
00:06:36,450 --> 00:06:36,630
Okay.

148
00:06:36,830 --> 00:06:42,850
So this phrase, you can usually use it when you meet somebody for the first time.

149
00:06:43,030 --> 00:06:44,450
I've heard so much about you.

150
00:06:44,710 --> 00:06:45,090
That's right.

151
00:06:45,270 --> 00:06:47,710
So the longer version is I've heard so much about you.

152
00:06:48,150 --> 00:06:51,770
Basically you're saying, listen, Cindy talks about you a lot.

153
00:06:52,230 --> 00:06:53,670
I've heard really nice things.

154
00:06:54,370 --> 00:06:55,590
Sometimes people like to joke.

155
00:06:55,850 --> 00:06:57,310
You say, I've heard so much about you.

156
00:06:57,550 --> 00:06:59,430
They say, I hope only good things.

157
00:06:59,560 --> 00:07:00,880
Yeah, I hope not too much.

158
00:07:00,880 --> 00:07:01,870
I hope not too much.

159
00:07:02,270 --> 00:07:07,250
So this is a very polite way to respond when someone introduces themselves.

160
00:07:07,720 --> 00:07:08,720
Okay.

161
00:07:08,720 --> 00:07:09,690
I've heard so much about you.

162
00:07:09,880 --> 00:07:11,610
It's a pleasure to finally meet you.

163
00:07:11,830 --> 00:07:12,310
Exactly.

164
00:07:13,200 --> 00:07:14,200
Okay.

165
00:07:14,200 --> 00:07:18,920
So they started talking and actually Thurston offers him a drink.

166
00:07:18,920 --> 00:07:19,970
He says, well, what do you want?

167
00:07:20,400 --> 00:07:21,840
Whiskey, bourbon?

168
00:07:21,910 --> 00:07:22,510
Pick your poison.

169
00:07:23,210 --> 00:07:23,770
All right.

170
00:07:23,840 --> 00:07:26,430
This is one of my very favorite phrases in English.

171
00:07:27,320 --> 00:07:31,340
The father-in-law wants to give the new son-in-law a drink.

172
00:07:31,340 --> 00:07:32,810
And there are many different options.

173
00:07:33,510 --> 00:07:35,870
So pick your poison means pick your drink.

174
00:07:36,470 --> 00:07:37,430
You might hear this at a bar.

175
00:07:37,990 --> 00:07:38,550
What do you want to drink?

176
00:07:38,770 --> 00:07:39,430
Pick your poison.

177
00:07:39,960 --> 00:07:40,960
Right.

178
00:07:40,960 --> 00:07:41,470
You could have whiskey, rum.

179
00:07:42,070 --> 00:07:44,850
So usually we're just talking about alcoholic drinks.

180
00:07:45,070 --> 00:07:45,270
Okay.

181
00:07:46,040 --> 00:07:51,920
So you wouldn't really use this for juices or water or something like that?

182
00:07:51,920 --> 00:07:52,920
Nah.

183
00:07:52,920 --> 00:07:53,920
This is for alcohol.

184
00:07:53,920 --> 00:07:54,920
Okay.

185
00:07:54,920 --> 00:07:57,360
So if you say pick your poison, that's for alcohol, right?

186
00:07:57,360 --> 00:07:59,350
Or maybe you can ask somebody what they're drinking.

187
00:07:59,640 --> 00:08:01,230
You would say, what's your poison?

188
00:08:01,520 --> 00:08:02,520
Yeah.

189
00:08:02,520 --> 00:08:02,710
What's your poison?

190
00:08:03,520 --> 00:08:04,520
Okay.

191
00:08:04,520 --> 00:08:06,840
So poison in this case is a slang term for alcohol.

192
00:08:06,840 --> 00:08:07,010
Very good.

193
00:08:08,040 --> 00:08:18,040
And finally, when Cindy was talking about lemonade that her mother made, she said she makes it from scratch, to make something from scratch.

194
00:08:18,840 --> 00:08:22,140
You could say, my mother makes chocolate chip cookies from scratch.

195
00:08:22,440 --> 00:08:27,540
This means not to buy something in the store, but to make it all yourself from nothing.

196
00:08:28,350 --> 00:08:34,070
So making lemonade from scratch means taking lemons, cutting them, taking the juice, adding some sugar.

197
00:08:34,210 --> 00:08:35,630
So you're making it from nothing.

198
00:08:36,430 --> 00:08:42,550
You're not using some kind of kit or some kind of canned drink from the store.

199
00:08:42,880 --> 00:08:43,880
Okay.

200
00:08:43,880 --> 00:08:50,950
So for example, maybe some Italians in Italy make their spaghetti from scratch with flour and they knead it and they make everything from scratch.

201
00:08:51,320 --> 00:08:52,320
Wow.

202
00:08:52,320 --> 00:08:55,650
So you don't buy a box of spaghetti, but you actually have to cut the spaghetti and pull it.

203
00:08:56,200 --> 00:08:57,200
Exactly.

204
00:08:57,200 --> 00:08:57,900
Very, very hard work.

205
00:08:58,200 --> 00:08:59,340
So to make something from scratch.

206
00:08:59,840 --> 00:09:02,480
And usually you can add the something goes in between, right?

207
00:09:02,680 --> 00:09:06,100
To make lemonade from scratch, make bread from scratch.

208
00:09:06,520 --> 00:09:07,400
Make ice cream from scratch.

209
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:09,000
Okay.

210
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:11,320
So those are our three phrases for today.

211
00:09:11,320 --> 00:09:16,540
Let's listen to the dialogue for one last time and we'll be back to talk about this very, very interesting topic.

212
00:09:25,830 --> 00:09:28,970
Mother, father, I'd like to introduce you to my fiance, Bob.

213
00:09:29,480 --> 00:09:30,210
Hello, Bob.

214
00:09:30,550 --> 00:09:30,670
Welcome.

215
00:09:31,350 --> 00:09:32,330
Thanks for having me.

216
00:09:32,480 --> 00:09:33,920
Uh, nice to meet the both of you.

217
00:09:33,950 --> 00:09:34,770
I've heard so much.

218
00:09:35,720 --> 00:09:40,240
So Cindy told you about bringing home her last boyfriend then.

219
00:09:40,240 --> 00:09:40,440
That idiot.

220
00:09:41,240 --> 00:09:42,240
Shhh.

221
00:09:42,240 --> 00:09:43,240
Thurston, you're going to scare the poor boy.

222
00:09:43,540 --> 00:09:44,500
Come in and have a drink.

223
00:09:45,240 --> 00:09:46,400
Dinner will be on in just a bit.

224
00:09:46,400 --> 00:09:47,960
What will you be having?

225
00:09:47,960 --> 00:09:49,800
Whiskey, bourbon, pick your poison.

226
00:09:50,720 --> 00:09:52,880
If you have a lemonade, that'd be great.

227
00:09:53,320 --> 00:09:54,320
Lemonade?

228
00:09:54,320 --> 00:09:56,200
Why sure, there's some in the fridge.

229
00:09:56,200 --> 00:09:57,620
Mother makes her own lemonade from scratch.

230
00:09:58,360 --> 00:09:58,720
It's the best.

231
00:09:59,360 --> 00:10:00,760
So, what are your hobbies, son?

232
00:10:01,160 --> 00:10:02,900
If you want, we can do some hunting tomorrow.

233
00:10:03,420 --> 00:10:05,800
I've just picked up a new rifle I've been meaning to try out.

234
00:10:06,120 --> 00:10:07,120
Should be a real hoot.

235
